<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">

<head>
<%- include("partials/head", { title: title, mapPage: true }) %>
</head>

<body>
<%- include("partials/nav", { active: "explore" }) %>

<%
  var firstYear = Number(abilityYears[0]);
  var lastYear = Number(abilityYears[abilityYears.length - 1]);
  var yearPos = function (y) { return ((Number(y) - firstYear) / (lastYear - firstYear)) * 100; };
%>
    <%# Shared SVG defs: the hatch patterns for the maps and charts, added
        by map.js. %>
    <svg class="filters" width="0" height="0" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
        <defs id="hatches"></defs>
    </svg>

    <div class="page" id="top">
        <%# 01 · Introduction. The 32 counties (D3) stay pinned while the title
            and four sentences scroll past: they fly apart into a grid ranked
            by their 1851 answer, take on their colours, then fly back
            together as the 1851 map. %>
        <section class="opener" id="opener" data-stage="0">
            <%# The title is pinned together with the island, so it doesn't slide
                over the map; it fades out when the first sentence arrives. %>
            <div class="opener-graphic">
                <div class="opener-map" id="opener-map" aria-hidden="true"></div>
                <header class="hero">
                    <h1>Where did the Irish language go?</h1>
                    <p class="dek">An interactive history of Irish speakers, <%= firstYear %>&ndash;<%= lastYear %></p>
                    <%# The hand-drawn scroll cue (VectorCraft). Tapping it glides to
                        the first sentence; if nobody scrolls, the screen gives a
                        small lift after a few seconds (map.js). %>
                    <button class="hero-scroll" id="hero-scroll" type="button" aria-label="Scroll down to begin">
                        <img src="img/scroll-cue.svg" width="64" height="64" alt="">
                        <span>scroll</span>
                    </button>
                </header>
            </div>

            <div class="opener-steps">
                <div class="step opener-spacer" data-opener="0" aria-hidden="true"></div>
                <div class="step opener-step" data-opener="1">
                    <p>In <%= firstYear %>, the census asked everyone in Ireland&rsquo;s 32 counties the same question: <em>can you speak Irish?</em></p>
                </div>
                <div class="step opener-step" data-opener="2">
                    <p>In the <span class="rough-mark" data-colour="#6fb78c">west</span>, most people said yes. In Galway, 69.1%. In Mayo, 65.8%. In Kerry, 61.5%.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="step opener-step" data-opener="3">
                    <p>On the <span class="rough-mark" data-colour="#dbe9cc">east coast</span>, almost nobody did. In Wicklow, 0.1%. In Dublin, 1.2%.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="step opener-step" data-opener="4">
                    <p>Two Irelands. This is the story of how that map changed over the next 170 years, and what &ldquo;speaking Irish&rdquo; has come to mean.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <%# 02 · Change over time: the average line draws itself, pausing at
            census years. History is given as context, not as a cause. %>
        <section class="scrolly scrolly-chart">
            <div class="chapter-head">
                <img class="spot" src="img/spot-decline.svg" alt="">
                <h2 class="section-title">The long decline</h2>
            </div>
            <div class="scrolly-graphic">
                <figure class="story-figure">
                    <div class="story-chart" id="story-chart" data-step="1"></div>
                    <figcaption class="chart-caption" id="story-caption">
                        Average of the 26 counties of the Republic, which have a figure in every census.
                    </figcaption>
                </figure>
            </div>

            <div class="scrolly-steps">
                <div class="step" data-story="1">
                    <p>In <strong>1851</strong>, across the 26 counties that are now the Republic, the average county share was <strong>22.1%</strong>.</p>
                    <p class="step-note">The census was taken as the Great Famine (1845&ndash;1852) was ending.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="step" data-story="2">
                    <p>Then it fell, census after census, for sixty years, to its lowest point in <strong>1911: 13.9%</strong>.</p>
                    <p class="step-note">The Gaelic League was founded in 1893 to revive the language. The line had not yet turned when the 1911 census was taken.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="step" data-story="3">
                    <p>After independence it turned. By <strong>2002</strong> the average had more than tripled, to <strong>43.6%</strong>.</p>
                    <p class="step-note">In 1922 the new Free State placed Irish at the centre of the national-school day. These dates are context: the census alone can&rsquo;t tell us what caused the change.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="step" data-story="4">
                    <p>The counties also came together. In 1851, <strong>Galway</strong> and <span class="rough-mark" data-colour="#bfe2df">Wicklow</span> were <strong>69 points</strong> apart.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="step" data-story="5">
                    <p>By 2022 they were <strong>9.8 points</strong> apart. The widest gap between any two Republic counties is now 13.6 points.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="step" data-story="6">
                    <p>One jump stands out. From <strong>1991 to 1996</strong> the average rose 9.1 points, from 33.0% to 42.1%, in five years.</p>
                    <p class="step-note">That is fast for a language. Treat it with some caution: a jump like this can come from how a question is asked or answered.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <%# 03 · Geography: the map stays pinned while the steps move it
            through the census years. %>
        <section class="scrolly scrolly-map">
            <div class="chapter-head">
                <img class="spot" src="img/island.svg" alt="">
                <h2 class="section-title">A changing landscape</h2>
            </div>
            <div class="scrolly-graphic">
                <div class="timeline" id="timeline">
                    <%# Follows the scroll steps, and is also a control: tap or drag
                        it (or use the arrow keys) to pick any census. %>
                    <div class="timeline-track" id="timeline-track" role="slider" tabindex="0"
                        aria-label="Census year" aria-valuemin="<%= firstYear %>" aria-valuemax="<%= lastYear %>" aria-valuenow="<%= defaultYear %>">
                        <span class="timeline-rail"></span>
                        <span class="timeline-fill" id="timeline-fill"></span>
                        <% abilityYears.forEach((year) => { %>
                        <span class="timeline-stop" data-year="<%= year %>" style="left:<%= yearPos(year).toFixed(3) %>%"></span>
                        <% }); %>
                        <span class="timeline-handle" id="timeline-handle" style="left:<%= yearPos(defaultYear).toFixed(3) %>%">
                            <span class="timeline-year" id="timeline-year"><%= defaultYear %></span>
                        </span>
                        <span class="timeline-axis" aria-hidden="true">
                            <span style="left:0"><%= firstYear %></span>
                            <% [1900, 1950, 2000].forEach((y) => { %>
                            <span style="left:<%= yearPos(y).toFixed(3) %>%"><%= y %></span>
                            <% }); %>
                            <span style="left:100%"><%= lastYear %></span>
                        </span>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="map-wrap">
                    <%# The line for the map (inside, so on desktop it can sit over
                        the map's top edge without changing the map's size). %>
                    <h2 class="finding" id="finding" aria-live="polite"></h2>
                    <div id="map"></div>
                </div>

                <div id="legend" class="map-legend"></div>
            </div>

            <div class="scrolly-steps">
                <%# Each step makes one point about where Irish lived and why that
                    changed; the note under it gives the history as context. The
                    line above the map (map.js, MAP_HEADLINES) states the lesson
                    with figures worked out from the data. %>
                <div class="step" data-year="1851" data-headline="west">
                    <p>In <strong>1851</strong>, the darker the green, the more people spoke Irish. It lived along the <strong>west and south coasts</strong>.</p>
                    <p class="step-note">The east, with its towns and trade with Britain, had already moved to English.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="step" data-year="1851" data-county="GALWAY" data-headline="galway" data-focus="GALWAY,MAYO,CLARE,ROSCOMMON,SLIGO,LEITRIM">
                    <p><strong>Galway</strong> led at 69.1%. This first census to ask about Irish came just as the <strong>Great Famine</strong> ended.</p>
                    <p class="step-note">Hunger and emigration fell heavily on the countryside, where Irish was strongest.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="step" data-year="1911" data-county="MAYO" data-headline="mayo" data-focus="MAYO,GALWAY,SLIGO,ROSCOMMON,DONEGAL,LEITRIM">
                    <p>By 1911 the heartland had faded: <strong>Mayo</strong> fell from 65.8% to 46.1%.</p>
                    <p class="step-note">From 1831 the national schools taught in English. Historians debate how much that mattered.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="step" data-year="1926" data-headline="north-gap" data-focus="ANTRIM,DOWN,ARMAGH,TYRONE,FERMANAGH,LONDONDERRY">
                    <p>Then the six <strong>Northern Ireland</strong> counties turn grey, until 1991.</p>
                    <p class="step-note">The Northern census dropped the question in 1926. Grey means no record, not no speakers.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="step" data-year="1961" data-county="DUBLIN" data-headline="dublin" data-focus="DUBLIN,WICKLOW,KILDARE,MEATH,LOUTH,CARLOW">
                    <p><strong>Dublin</strong> rose from 3.7% in 1911 to 21.1% in 1961, while Galway kept falling.</p>
                    <p class="step-note">From 1922 Irish was compulsory in national schools. The census can&rsquo;t prove a link, but the timing is hard to miss.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="step" data-year="1981" data-headline="slow">
                    <p>The next decades were slow: by <strong>1981</strong> the east had crept up and the heartland had slipped.</p>
                    <p class="step-note">Dublin gained 7 points and Wicklow 5, while Galway lost 3.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="step" data-year="1991" data-headline="north-returns" data-focus="ANTRIM,DOWN,ARMAGH,TYRONE,FERMANAGH,LONDONDERRY">
                    <p>In <strong>1991</strong> the North reappears, for the first time since 1911, and is the palest on the map.</p>
                    <p class="step-note">The question returned after the Anglo-Irish Agreement of 1985.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="step" data-year="1996" data-headline="jump">
                    <p>In <strong>1996</strong> the whole Republic turned green at once.</p>
                    <p class="step-note">That year the CSO brought in a new question, so part of the leap may be the wording.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="step" data-year="2022" data-county="WICKLOW" data-headline="wicklow" data-focus="WICKLOW,DUBLIN,WEXFORD,CARLOW,KILDARE,KILKENNY">
                    <p>By 2022 <strong>Wicklow</strong>, once at 0.1%, was at 39.0%. The old west&ndash;east divide had gone.</p>
                    <p class="step-note">The Republic peaked in 2002. The sharpest line left is the border.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="step" data-year="2022">
                    <p>Now explore: <strong>drag the timeline</strong>, or <strong>tap a county</strong>.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section class="column section north-south">
            <div class="chapter-head">
                <img class="spot spot-wide" src="img/spot-two-jurisdictions.svg" alt="">
                <h2 class="section-title">Two jurisdictions</h2>
            </div>
            <p class="ns-lead">
                In the Republic, about <strong>4 in 10</strong> people say they can
                speak Irish. In Northern Ireland, <strong>fewer than 1 in 10</strong>.
            </p>
            <%# Two ranges side by side, then every county ranked, in the map's
                colours. Figures: latest census, 2022 in the Republic and 2021 in
                Northern Ireland. %>
            <div class="ns-compare" id="ns-compare"></div>
            <div class="ns-bars" id="north-south" role="img"
                aria-label="Bar chart of every county in the latest census: Republic counties from 48.8% (Galway) to 35.2% (Louth), Northern Ireland counties from 10.0% (Tyrone) to 4.1% (Down), with no county between 10% and 35%."></div>
            <%# Phones: the chart above is a pile of falling dots; tapping a dot
                names it here (map.js). %>
            <p class="ns-readout" aria-live="polite"></p>
            <p class="chart-caption">
                Latest census: 2022 in the Republic, 2021 in Northern Ireland, which
                runs its own census.
            </p>
        </section>

        <%# 04 · Interpretation: 100 figures for 100 people, after The
            Pudding's unit charts. Figures from the CSO's 2022 summary. %>
        <section class="scrolly scrolly-people">
            <div class="chapter-head">
                <img class="spot" src="img/spot-speaker.svg" alt="">
                <h2 class="section-title">What counts as speaking Irish?</h2>
            </div>
            <div class="scrolly-graphic">
                <figure class="people-figure">
                    <p class="people-readout" id="people-readout"></p>
                    <div class="people" id="people" data-stage="1" role="img"
                        aria-label="100 figures representing 100 people aged 3 and over in 2022"></div>
                    <figcaption class="chart-caption">
                        Each figure is about 1 in 100 people aged 3 and over in the Republic, 2022.
                        Source: <a href="https://www.cso.ie/en/releasesandpublications/ep/p-cpsr/censusofpopulation2022-summaryresults/educationandirishlanguage/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CSO, Census 2022 summary results</a>.
                    </figcaption>
                </figure>
            </div>

            <div class="scrolly-steps">
                <div class="step" data-people="1">
                    <p>Everything so far counts people who <em>said they could</em> speak Irish. In 2022 that was <strong>1,873,997 people</strong>: about <strong>40 in every 100</strong>.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="step" data-people="2">
                    <p>But being able to isn&rsquo;t the same as using it. One in four of them, <strong>472,887 people</strong>, said they <strong>never</strong> speak it.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="step" data-people="3">
                    <p>A third, <strong>623,961</strong>, speak it every day. Most of them are pupils, speaking it at school.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="step" data-people="4">
                    <p>Outside the education system, <strong>71,968 people</strong> speak Irish every day. That is fewer than <strong>2 in every 100</strong>.</p>
                    <p class="step-note">So the census measures ability, as reported by each household. The map shows where people say they can speak Irish, not where it is spoken.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <%# 05 · Exploration: guess, then compare a county with the trend. %>
        <section class="county-detail column section" id="county-detail">
            <div class="chapter-head">
                <img class="spot" src="img/spot-pin.svg" alt="">
                <h2 class="section-title">Find your county</h2>
            </div>
            <p class="section-sub">
                Pick a county, slide to your guess for <%= firstYear %>, then reveal
                the answer and the county&rsquo;s whole history.
            </p>
            <div class="guess-form">
                <label class="guess-field">
                    <span>County</span>
                    <select id="guess-county"></select>
                </label>
                <label class="guess-field guess-slider">
                    <span>Your guess for <%= firstYear %>: <output id="guess-output">35%</output></span>
                    <input type="range" id="guess-range" min="0" max="70" step="1" value="35">
                </label>
                <button type="button" class="button" id="guess-reveal">Reveal</button>
            </div>
            <%# Drawn empty from the start (your guess, a "?" for the answer and
                blank axes), then filled in by the reveal. D3, in map.js. %>
            <figure class="guess-card">
                <div class="guess-chart" id="guess-chart"></div>
                <p class="guess-reaction" id="guess-reaction" aria-live="polite"></p>
            </figure>
        </section>

        <%# 06 · Conclusion: back to the opening question, with the two maps
            side by side. %>
        <section class="column section conclusion">
            <div class="chapter-head">
                <img class="spot" src="img/spot-harp.svg" alt="">
                <h2 class="section-title">So, where did the Irish language go?</h2>
            </div>
            <p class="answer">
                It didn't vanish. <strong>It spread out.</strong>
            </p>
            <%# Both maps are sketched in the browser with rough.js from the
                county shapes, on the same colour scale as the main map. %>
            <figure class="sketch-maps">
                <div class="sketch-maps-row" id="sketch-maps" role="img"
                    aria-label="Two sketched maps of Ireland. 1851: dark in the west, almost blank in the east. 2022: an even mid green across the Republic, pale in Northern Ireland."></div>
                <figcaption class="sketch-key" id="sketch-key"></figcaption>
            </figure>
<%# The three takeaways as hand-drawn stat cards: then, now, and the
                catch. They drop in one after another (map.js). %>
            <ol class="findings">
                <li class="finding-card">
                    <span class="finding-tag">1851</span>
                    <span class="finding-stat">69 points</span>
                    <p>between <strong>Galway</strong> (69.1%) and <strong>Wicklow</strong> (0.1%). Irish was the language of the west.</p>
                </li>
                <li class="finding-card">
                    <span class="finding-tag">2022</span>
                    <span class="finding-stat">35&ndash;49%</span>
                    <p>in every county of the Republic. The language had <strong>spread out</strong> evenly.</p>
                </li>
                <li class="finding-card is-catch">
                    <span class="finding-tag">But</span>
                    <span class="finding-stat">under 2%</span>
                    <p>of people speak it <strong>daily outside school</strong>. Being able to speak Irish isn&rsquo;t the same as using it.</p>
                </li>
            </ol>
            <p class="chart-caption">The figures are self-reported and the census questions changed over time, so read the long trend as a guide, not a precise measurement.</p>
        </section>

        <aside class="closing" lang="ga">
            <p class="closing-line"><span id="closing-proverb">Tír gan teanga, tír gan anam.</span></p>
            <p class="closing-gloss" lang="en">&ldquo;A country without a language is a country without a soul.&rdquo; Attributed to Patrick Pearse.</p>
        </aside>

<%- include("partials/footer") %>
    </div>

<script src="vendor/leaflet/leaflet.js"></script>
<script src="vendor/gsap.min.js"></script>
<script src="vendor/ScrollToPlugin.min.js"></script>
<script src="vendor/d3.min.js"></script>
<script src="vendor/rough.js"></script>
<%# RoughNotation: the hand-drawn highlights (a step's marked words, the
    closing proverb). %>
<script src="vendor/rough-notation.iife.js"></script>
<script src="js/map.js?v=<%= assetVersion %>"></script>
<script src="js/nav.js?v=<%= assetVersion %>"></script>

</body>

</html>
